Issue title: reverse_proxy: http transport strips zero'd content-length
Issue body excerpt: ### Issue Details

*Disclaimer*: I haven't fully narrowed this down to being an issue with Caddy yet, so anyone confirming my findings would be appreciated.

From what it looks like, when presented with an HTTP request (*HEAD* and *GET* were tested) without body, any *content-length* headers set to 0 (zero) are removed.
This causes issues when operating, say, a Ceph radosgw or other S3 provider when a signed request is sent which includes the *content-length* header in its *SignedHeaders*[^signed-headers].
With the header removed the signature fails.

[^signed-headers]: e.g. `SignedHeaders=content-length;host;x-amz-content-sha256;x-amz-date`

<details><summary>stripped down configuration</summary>

```caddyfile
{
  key_type p384
  # ACME and TLS config here
}
(trusted-headers) {
  request_header -x-forwarded-for
  request_header -x-forwarded-proto
  request_header -forwarded
}
example.com {
  import trusted-headers
  tls /run/credentials/caddy.service/rgw_cert.pem /run/credentials/caddy.service/rgw_key.pem
  reverse_proxy [::1]:7480
}
https:// {
  import trusted-headers
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  reverse_proxy [::1]:7480 {
    transport http {
      compression off # for testing, didn't make a difference
    }
  }
}
```

</details>

Command used to test:

```bash
curl --http1.1 -s -o /dev/null -vv -I -H "Content-Length: 0" "https://example.com/my-bucket"
```

Since I've *tcpdump*'d the traffic on port 7480 (the backend) I am reasonably sure that this does *not* include a *content-length* header in the request to the upstream.
